LeetCode 199 — Binary Tree Right Side View
Problem Understanding

You are given a binary tree and need to return the nodes that would be visible if the tree were viewed from the right side.

For every level, we need exactly one node:

the rightmost visible node at that level.

Example
                 1
               /   \
              2     3
               \     \
                5     4

From the right side:

Level 1 → 1
Level 2 → 3
Level 3 → 4

Answer:

[1, 3, 4]

The problem is essentially:

Binary Tree
     ↓
Process level by level
     ↓
Take the rightmost node of every level

So BFS is the most natural solution.

Approach 1 — Brute Force: DFS + Find Maximum Depth/Rightmost Node

A brute-force way is to find the visible node separately for each level.

For each depth:

depth = 0
depth = 1
depth = 2
...

we traverse the entire tree and keep the rightmost node encountered at that depth.

This repeats work, so it is less efficient.

Code
import java.util.*;

class Solution {

    static class Info {
        int value;
        int position;

        Info(int value, int position) {
            this.value = value;
            this.position = position;
        }
    }

    int maxDepth(TreeNode root) {
        if (root == null) {
            return -1;
        }

        return 1 + Math.max(
                maxDepth(root.left),
                maxDepth(root.right)
        );
    }

    void findRightmost(
            TreeNode root,
            int depth,
            int targetDepth,
            int[] count,
            Info result) {

        if (root == null) {
            return;
        }

        if (depth == targetDepth) {
            count[0]++;

            if (count[0] > result.position) {
                result.position = count[0];
                result.value = root.val;
            }
        }

        findRightmost(
                root.left,
                depth + 1,
                targetDepth,
                count,
                result
        );

        findRightmost(
                root.right,
                depth + 1,
                targetDepth,
                count,
                result
        );
    }

    public List<Integer> rightSideView(TreeNode root) {

        List<Integer> answer = new ArrayList<>();

        if (root == null) {
            return answer;
        }

        int height = maxDepth(root);

        for (int level = 0; level <= height; level++) {

            int[] count = new int[1];

            Info result = new Info(-1, -1);

            findRightmost(
                    root,
                    0,
                    level,
                    count,
                    result
            );

            answer.add(result.value);
        }

        return answer;
    }
}

