Approach 2 — DFS + Store Rightmost Node at Each Level

We can improve the brute force idea by visiting the tree only once.

For every depth, store the first node we encounter when traversing:

right → left

Why?

Because the first node encountered at a level will be the rightmost node.

Example

Instead of:

left → right

we do:

right → left

For:

        1
       / \
      2   3
         / \
        6   7

DFS order:

1 → 3 → 7 → 6 → 2

The first node at every level:

level 0 → 1
level 1 → 3
level 2 → 7

Exactly the right-side view.

Code
import java.util.*;

class Solution {

    public List<Integer> rightSideView(TreeNode root) {

        List<Integer> answer = new ArrayList<>();

        dfs(root, 0, answer);

        return answer;
    }

    private void dfs(
            TreeNode root,
            int depth,
            List<Integer> answer) {

        if (root == null) {
            return;
        }

        // First node visited at this level
        // is the rightmost node.
        if (depth == answer.size()) {
            answer.add(root.val);
        }

        // Visit right first
        dfs(root.right, depth + 1, answer);

        // Then left
        dfs(root.left, depth + 1, answer);
    }
}

