LeetCode 101 — Symmetric Tree
Problem Understanding

A binary tree is symmetric if its left and right subtrees are mirror images of each other.

For every pair of corresponding nodes:

left subtree                right subtree
     ↓                           ↓
left child   ↔   right child
right child  ↔   left child

For example:

                 1
               /   \
              2     2
             / \   / \
            3   4 4   3

This is symmetric:

           1
         /   \
        2     2
       / \   / \
      3   4 4   3

Answer:

true

But:

                 1
               /   \
              2     2
             /       \
            3         3

This is also symmetric.

Whereas:

                 1
               /   \
              2     2
             /       \
            3         4

is not symmetric because:

3 != 4
The Core Idea

The most important thing to understand is:

We do not compare left subtree with left subtree and right subtree with right subtree.

We compare:

left.left   ↔   right.right
left.right  ↔   right.left

So for two nodes root1 and root2 to be mirrors:

1. Both null → true
2. One null → false
3. Values different → false
4. root1.left  ↔ root2.right
5. root1.right ↔ root2.left

This is the key pattern behind the whole problem.

Approach 1 — Brute Force: Mirror Traversals + Compare
Idea

One way to think about symmetry is:

Left subtree

should be exactly the mirror image of:

Right subtree

So we can generate:

normal preorder of the left subtree
mirror-preorder of the right subtree

and compare them.

Important

We must include null markers.

Otherwise, different structures can produce the same sequence.

For example:

Tree A:        2
              /
             3

Tree B:        2
               \
                3

Normal values:

[2,3]
[2,3]

So values alone are insufficient.

Using null:

Tree A → [2,3,null,null,null]
Tree B → [2,null,3,null,null]

Now they differ.

Code
class Solution {

    public boolean isSymmetric(TreeNode root) {

        if (root == null) {
            return true;
        }

        List<Integer> left = new ArrayList<>();
        List<Integer> right = new ArrayList<>();

        normalPreorder(root.left, left);
        mirrorPreorder(root.right, right);

        return left.equals(right);
    }

    // Root -> Left -> Right
    private void normalPreorder(
            TreeNode root,
            List<Integer> list) {

        if (root == null) {
            list.add(null);
            return;
        }

        list.add(root.val);

        normalPreorder(root.left, list);
        normalPreorder(root.right, list);
    }

    // Root -> Right -> Left
    private void mirrorPreorder(
            TreeNode root,
            List<Integer> list) {

        if (root == null) {
            list.add(null);
            return;
        }

        list.add(root.val);

        mirrorPreorder(root.right, list);
        mirrorPreorder(root.left, list);
    }
}
