Approach 2 — Recursive Mirror Comparison

This is the most natural way to solve the problem.

Instead of generating traversals, directly compare the two subtrees as mirrors.

Define:

isChecking(root1, root2)

to mean:

Are root1 and root2 mirror images?

Then the rules are straightforward.

Rule 1 — Both null
if (root1 == null && root2 == null)
    return true;

Example:

      null     null

There is nothing to compare.

Rule 2 — One is null
if (root1 == null || root2 == null)
    return false;

Example:

      3        null

Structures don't match.

Rule 3 — Values differ
if (root1.val != root2.val)
    return false;
Rule 4 — Compare opposite children

This is the most important line:

isChecking(root1.left, root2.right)
&&
isChecking(root1.right, root2.left)

Notice:

LEFT  ↔ RIGHT
RIGHT ↔ LEFT

not:

LEFT  ↔ LEFT
RIGHT ↔ RIGHT
