Why the Recursive Solution Is So Elegant

The definition of symmetry itself is recursive:

A tree is symmetric if:

left subtree and right subtree
are mirror images.

And two subtrees are mirrors if:

root values equal
+
left of first = right of second
+
right of first = left of second

This directly translates into:

if (root1 == null && root2 == null)
    return true;

if (root1 == null || root2 == null)
    return false;

if (root1.val != root2.val)
    return false;

return isChecking(root1.left, root2.right)
    && isChecking(root1.right, root2.left);

That's why this is the solution you should remember.

Approach 3 — Iterative BFS Using Queue of Pairs

We can perform the same mirror comparison iteratively.

Instead of recursion, put pairs of nodes into a queue:

(root.left, root.right)

Then repeatedly compare the pair.

For each matching pair:

(left1, right2)
(right1, left2)

are added.

Code
class Pair {
    TreeNode first;
    TreeNode second;

    Pair(TreeNode first, TreeNode second) {
        this.first = first;
        this.second = second;
    }
}

class Solution {

    public boolean isSymmetric(TreeNode root) {

        if (root == null) {
            return true;
        }

        Queue<Pair> q = new LinkedList<>();

        q.offer(new Pair(root.left, root.right));

        while (!q.isEmpty()) {

            Pair curr = q.poll();

            TreeNode first = curr.first;
            TreeNode second = curr.second;

            // Both null
            if (first == null && second == null) {
                continue;
            }

            // One null
            if (first == null || second == null) {
                return false;
            }

            // Different values
            if (first.val != second.val) {
                return false;
            }

            // Mirror comparison
            q.offer(new Pair(first.left, second.right));
            q.offer(new Pair(first.right, second.left));
        }

        return true;
    }
}
