Approach 5 — Level-by-Level Comparison

Another way to understand symmetry is to process each level and make sure it reads the same from both directions.

For example:

Level 0:
[1]

Level 1:
[2,2]

Level 2:
[3,4,4,3]

Each level must be a palindrome.

However, we must include null positions to preserve structure.

This can be implemented using BFS.

Code
class Solution {

    public boolean isSymmetric(TreeNode root) {

        if (root == null) {
            return true;
        }

        Queue<TreeNode> q = new LinkedList<>();
        q.offer(root);

        while (!q.isEmpty()) {

            int size = q.size();

            List<Integer> level = new ArrayList<>();

            for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {

                TreeNode curr = q.poll();

                if (curr == null) {
                    level.add(null);
                    continue;
                }

                level.add(curr.val);

                q.offer(curr.left);
                q.offer(curr.right);
            }

            // Check palindrome
            int left = 0;
            int right = level.size() - 1;

            while (left < right) {

                if (!Objects.equals(
                        level.get(left),
                        level.get(right))) {

                    return false;
                }

                left++;
                right--;
            }

            // Remove trailing null-only levels
            boolean allNull = true;

            for (Integer value : level) {
                if (value != null) {
                    allNull = false;
                    break;
                }
            }

            if (allNull) {
                break;
            }
        }

        return true;
    }
}

This approach is useful for visualizing the palindrome property, but it carries extra bookkeeping and is less clean than directly comparing mirror node pairs.

